# ...and the siblings the state code writes next to each of them: `.bak`
# (save_seen_articles / save_replied_to), `.lock` (update_seen_articles_checked
# / update_replied_to) and `.corrupt.<timestamp>` (_load_json_with_repair_strict).
# Only the bare names were listed, so a test run left seen_articles.json.bak
# untracked-but-visible and `git add -A` swept it in (caught 2026-10-19).
#
# A committed .bak is the dangerous one, and it defeats the exact guard the
# strict-read machinery exists for. On a runner the primary file is absent
# (gitignored), so `_load_json_with_repair_strict` restores from .bak and
# reports `from_file=True` — which makes `load_seen_articles_strict` call an
# EMPTY state trusted whenever the Gist read fails, instead of returning
# trusted=False and skipping the write. The next write then puts that empty
# state over the real Gist and erases every seen link, recent topic and Pioneer
# cooldown. This repo already fixed a stray .bak doing precisely that
# (2026-09-11); do not let one into the tree again.
seen_articles.json.*
replied_to.json.*
feed_health.json.*
post_metrics.json.*
growth.json.*
.claude/
docs/WATCHLIST_AUDIT.md

# Phase 4a watchlist candidates — real handles kept out of the public repo
scripts/watchlist_candidates.py

# Coverage artifacts (pytest-cov) — generated, never committed
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov/

# Flattened copies of the repo made for pasting into an external LLM for review
# (one <documents> blob of every source file). Regenerable, ~750 KB, and would
# be a duplicate of the repo inside the repo if `git add -A` ever swept it up.
repo-dump*.txt
